    const Section(
      title: 'History & Systems Review:',
      content: '**Functional Neurological Disorder (FND) — Key Historical Features**:\n- Acute or subacute onset of neurological symptoms that are incompatible with recognized neurological disease\n- Symptoms often follow a stressor (bullying, loss of friend, family disruption)\n- Extensive negative workup despite significant disability\n- History of high-achieving or high-performance background (competitive gymnast)\n- La belle indifference may or may not be present (its absence does not rule out FND)\n\n**Psychosocial Assessment (Critical in Pediatric FND)**:\n- **Precipitating stressors**: Best friend moved away, bullying at school, biological father incarcerated — multiple adverse childhood experiences (ACEs)\n- **Family dynamics**: Mother-stepfather relationship, custody situation, maternal anxiety and hypervigilance about medical diagnoses\n- **School performance**: Prior academic history, social relationships, any learning disabilities\n- **Psychiatric screening**: Assess for depression (PHQ-A), anxiety (GAD-7 or SCARED), PTSD, history of abuse or neglect\n- **Symptom modeling**: Has the patient observed wheelchair use or significant disability in family members or peers?\n- **Secondary gain assessment**: What has the wheelchair provided? (escape from school bullying, increased parental attention, avoidance of gymnastics pressure)\n- Important: secondary gain does not mean the patient is faking. FND is a genuine neurological disorder of brain network function, not malingering.\n\n**Pain Assessment**:\n- Bilateral leg heaviness and tingling — assess with Pediatric Pain Questionnaire\n- Pain catastrophizing in adolescents (Pain Catastrophizing Scale — Child version)\n- Functional Disability Inventory (FDI) — validated measure of pain-related disability in pediatrics\n- Sleep assessment: insomnia and pain amplification are commonly comorbid\n\n**Prior Treatment Review**:\n- Document all negative testing thoroughly (this will be important for parent education)\n- Review response to empiric immunologic treatment (none, supporting non-inflammatory etiology)\n- Assess for iatrogenic harm: unnecessary procedures, reinforcement of sick role through prolonged wheelchair use, homebound instruction reinforcing disability identity\n\n**Red Flags to Reassess**:\n- Progressive symptoms despite rehabilitation (consider re-evaluation)\n- New neurologic signs not consistent with FND (upper motor neuron signs, true muscle atrophy, optic neuritis)\n- Weight loss, fevers, night sweats\n- Bowel or bladder incontinence that is persistent and cannot be explained by functional patterns',
