      content: '**General**: Well-appearing, well-nourished 14-year-old girl seated in manual wheelchair. Appears comfortable at rest. Good eye contact. Appropriately interactive but becomes quiet when asked about school.\n\n**Vitals**: BP 110/68 mmHg, HR 78 bpm, BMI 21.5 kg/m2 (50th percentile). Afebrile.\n\n**Neurologic Examination — Positive Clinical Signs of FND**:\n- **Hoover sign (POSITIVE)**: When asked to extend the left hip against resistance, the hip extensors appear 2/5 strength. However, when asked to flex the right hip against resistance, the left hip extends with full force involuntarily (synergistic activation). Same finding on the right side. This is a validated positive clinical sign of functional weakness.\n- **Hip abductor sign (POSITIVE)**: When testing hip abduction, the contralateral hip (supposedly weak) demonstrates full abduction synergy. Inconsistent with organic weakness.\n- **Give-way weakness**: When testing knee extension, the patient initially demonstrates normal strength then suddenly releases. This "collapsing" pattern differs from true upper or lower motor neuron weakness patterns.\n- **Tremor entrainment**: When asked to tap a rhythm with her right hand, a bilateral lower extremity tremor that was present at rest ceases. This entrainment phenomenon is a positive sign of functional tremor.\n- **Drift without pronation**: When arms are extended with eyes closed, there is downward drift of the bilateral upper extremities without pronation. Organic upper motor neuron drift is accompanied by pronation.\n\n**Neurologic Examination — Conventional Findings**:\n- **Motor (voluntary testing)**: Bilateral hip flexors 2/5, hip extensors 2/5, knee extensors 3/5, knee flexors 3/5, ankle dorsiflexors 3/5, plantarflexors 3/5 (note: effort-dependent and inconsistent between trials)\n- **Functional motor**: Despite the above, when distracted during transfers, she was observed to briefly bear full weight through both legs before catching herself and sitting back down\n- **Sensory**: Reports decreased sensation to light touch in a non-dermatomal pattern (entire left leg to the groin crease, "splitting the midline" — a finding inconsistent with organic pathology as the midline has bilateral innervation)\n- **Reflexes**: 2+ and symmetric throughout. No clonus. Babinski downgoing bilaterally.\n- **Coordination**: Finger-to-nose intact. No cerebellar signs.\n- **Cranial nerves**: Intact throughout.\n\n**Musculoskeletal**: No contractures despite 6 months of wheelchair use (suggesting she is moving her legs more than reported). No muscle atrophy. Muscle bulk appears normal and symmetric.\n\n**Gait Assessment**: Patient states she cannot stand. With bilateral support and encouragement, she is able to stand with significant visible effort and truncal sway but does not collapse. Takes 3 steps with a slow, deliberate, effortful pattern that is not consistent with any specific neurologic gait pattern (not spastic, not ataxic, not steppage).',
