    const Section(
      title: '+ Explain the diagnostic criteria for FND, the significance of positive clinical signs, and why this is a "rule-in" diagnosis rather than a "rule-out" diagnosis.',
      content: '**Functional Neurological Disorder (FND) — Diagnostic Framework**:\n\n**DSM-5 Criteria (Conversion Disorder/FND)**:\n1. One or more symptoms of altered voluntary motor or sensory function\n2. Clinical findings provide evidence of **incompatibility** between the symptom and recognized neurological or medical conditions\n3. The symptom is not better explained by another medical or mental disorder\n4. The symptom causes clinically significant distress or impairment\n\n**Critical Concept — FND is a "Rule-In" Diagnosis**:\n- FND is NOT a diagnosis of exclusion. It is diagnosed based on **positive clinical signs** that demonstrate internal inconsistency.\n- Simply having a negative workup is insufficient and leads to diagnostic insecurity for patients and families\n- The positive signs found in this patient are validated, reliable clinical markers:\n\n**Positive Clinical Signs Found (and Their Significance)**:\n1. **Hoover sign**: Sensitivity 63%, specificity 100% for functional leg weakness. The involuntary synergistic activation of the "weak" hip extensors during contralateral hip flexion demonstrates that the motor pathways are intact and the weakness is not due to structural damage.\n2. **Hip abductor sign**: Same principle — contralateral synergistic activation during ipsilateral testing\n3. **Give-way (collapsing) weakness**: Inconsistent with pyramidal or lower motor neuron patterns. True weakness shows consistent force generation.\n4. **Tremor entrainment**: Disappearance of tremor during a distraction task proves the tremor is not generated by a fixed oscillator (as in Parkinson disease or essential tremor)\n5. **Midline splitting of sensory loss**: The midline has bilateral innervation; organic lesions do not cause sharp midline sensory boundaries\n6. **Drift without pronation**: Organic corticospinal tract dysfunction causes pronation with drift; drift without pronation suggests functional etiology\n7. **Observed weight-bearing during distracted transfer**: Demonstrates motor capability that is not accessible during formal testing\n\n**Why Further Testing is NOT Indicated**:\n- All appropriate testing has been completed and is negative\n- Positive clinical signs CONFIRM the diagnosis — they do not merely suggest it by exclusion\n- Additional testing carries risks: reinforcement of the sick role, iatrogenic anxiety, false-positive findings leading to inappropriate treatments, radiation exposure, procedural complications\n- Each additional test communicates to the patient: "We still think something might be structurally wrong"\n\n**Important Nuance**:\n- FND is a disorder of brain network function, not "imagined" or "faked"\n- fMRI studies show altered connectivity in the right temporoparietal junction and supplementary motor area in FND patients\n- It is as "real" as any other neurological condition and requires specialized treatment\n- Malingering is a conscious, deliberate fabrication of symptoms for external gain — FND is involuntary',
