    const Section(
      title: '+ Outline the interdisciplinary rehabilitation program for pediatric FND, including the critical first step of diagnosis communication.',
      content: '**Step 1 — Diagnosis Communication (The Most Important Step)**:\n- This conversation sets the trajectory for the entire treatment course. Done well, it creates buy-in. Done poorly, it alienates the family.\n- Meet with the patient and both parents together\n- Use language that is validating and explanatory, not dismissive:\n  - "Your daughter\'s weakness is real. Her brain is sending the wrong signals to her legs, like a software problem rather than a hardware problem. All of the hardware — the nerves, the muscles, the spinal cord — is working perfectly. But the signals are getting scrambled."\n  - "This is called Functional Neurological Disorder. We see this in young people, especially high-achieving, strong young people like your daughter. It is the brain\'s response to overwhelming stress."\n  - "The good news is that because the hardware is intact, the brain can relearn how to send the right signals. That is what rehabilitation is for, and children your daughter\'s age respond very well to this treatment."\n- Show the positive clinical signs to the family: demonstrate the Hoover sign in real time so they can see the legs working involuntarily\n- Acknowledge the stressors without forcing the patient to discuss them in detail: "Sometimes the brain protects us from overwhelming experiences by redirecting the stress into the body"\n\n**Step 2 — Inpatient Rehabilitation Program (2-3 Weeks)**:\n\n**Physical Therapy (Primary Treatment Modality)**:\n- Sessions: 60 minutes, 2x daily, 5 days per week\n- **Graded motor retraining**: Begin with the movements the patient can do (demonstrated during Hoover sign and distracted transfer). Build from automatic/involuntary movements to voluntary control.\n- **Distraction-based techniques**: Perform dual tasks (cognitive tasks, ball-catching, music) while walking to bypass the voluntary motor interference\n- **Treadmill training with body-weight support**: Allows the patient to walk with reduced fear of falling. Gradually decrease support as confidence builds.\n- **Mirror therapy and visual feedback**: Use mirrors to provide visual input of leg movement during involuntary activation\n- **Goal-setting**: Collaborative, patient-directed. Break walking into micro-goals (standing 30 seconds, 1 step, 5 steps, hallway, gym). Celebrate each milestone.\n- **Critical principle**: NEVER confront the patient about inconsistency. Never say, "We saw you move your legs, so we know you can walk." This is counterproductive and damages trust.\n\n**Psychology/Psychiatry (Concurrent)**:\n- CBT adapted for adolescents: cognitive restructuring of catastrophic thoughts about disability, behavioral activation\n- Process trauma and stressors: bullying, friend loss, father\'s incarceration. Trauma-informed care approach.\n- Address perfectionism and high-achievement pressure (common in gymnasts with FND)\n- Family therapy: address maternal anxiety, family dynamics with stepfather, communication patterns\n- Screen for and treat comorbid depression and anxiety (SSRI if indicated — fluoxetine is first-line in adolescents)\n\n**Occupational Therapy**:\n- ADL retraining progressing from wheelchair level to standing and ambulatory\n- Energy conservation and activity pacing\n- School task simulation (sitting at a desk, carrying books, navigating hallways)\n\n**School Reintegration Planning**:\n- Begin during inpatient stay with education liaison\n- Develop a graduated return-to-school plan (start with 2 hours, increase weekly)\n- 504 Plan or IEP accommodations: elevator access, extra transition time, rest breaks, counseling access\n- Coordinate with school counselor regarding bullying intervention\n\n**Recreational Therapy**:\n- Reintroduce physical activity in a low-pressure context\n- Group activities with peers on the rehab unit\n- Explore new interests if return to competitive gymnastics creates pressure\n\n**Pain Management**:\n- Avoid opioids — treat pain with CBT, PT, and non-pharmacologic strategies\n- Consider amitriptyline 10-25 mg at bedtime for pain and sleep\n- TENS unit for comfort during therapy\n\n**Discharge Criteria**:\n- Ambulating independently or with minimal device for household distances\n- Patient and family understand the diagnosis and the relapse prevention plan\n- Outpatient therapy arranged (PT 2x/week, psychology weekly)\n- School reintegration plan in place with specific dates\n- Follow-up with PM&R in 2 weeks, then monthly',
