ngs like elevated lactate, none of which your daughter has. More importantly, your daughter has positive clinical signs that confirm the diagnosis of FND. Let me show you."\n\n**Step 5 — Demonstrate a Positive Sign in Real Time**:\n"Watch what happens when I test her left leg. When I push down on her left leg and ask her to resist, it appears weak. But now watch — when I ask her to lift her right leg, her left leg pushes down with full force without her even trying. That tells me the pathway from her brain to her leg is working perfectly. The problem is in the voluntary control signal, not the nerve or muscle. This is a positive diagnostic test, not just a negative workup."\n\n**Step 6 — Reframe Testing as Potentially Harmful**:\n"I worry that more testing would actually set your daughter back. Every new test sends a message to her brain: the doctors still think something might be structurally wrong. That message keeps her stuck in the pattern. What her brain needs right now is the opposite message: we know what this is, we know how to treat it, and we believe in her ability to recover."\n\n**Step 7 — Propose a Collaborative Path Forward**:\n"Here is what I am asking of you. Give us three weeks. Let your daughter come into our rehabilitation program where we have a team that specializes in exactly this. If she is not making meaningful progress in three weeks, I will sit down with you and we will reassess together. But I have seen young people just like your daughter walk out of our program. I believe she can too. Can we try this together?"\n\n**Key Principles**:\n- Never say "it\'s all in her head" or "there\'s nothing wrong"\n- Demonstrating positive signs provides tangible evidence that is more convincing than verbal explanation\n- Setting a time-limited trial gives the anxious parent a concrete endpoint and reduces the pressure of an open-ended commitment\n- Partnering with the parent rather than opposing them is essential for treatment success',
